A||||27||onfire||I need a crash course in wine.||||1||
Z||000000||onfire||07-29-2001||02:00 PM||||Like what wine goes with what food? i.e. red meat, sea food, cheese and crackers. What are some good brands $40.00 or less? What are some good brands $40.00 or more? How do you store, than prepare wine? I could go on and on. So post anything and everything you know about wines. <P>       p.s. What does i.e. mean. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Uaana||07-29-2001||02:36 PM||||Could you be a little more specific?<BR>There a dessert wines. Dinner wines ect ect.<BR>As a rule Red goes with red meat and Italian food with red sauce.<BR>White wines go with fish and white meat foods.<BR>If you have somthing specific in mind just ask. <BR>Are you looking for a dry, sweet, full, light, there are so many choices I could go on for hours. If you want email me with any specific questions.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||07-29-2001||02:43 PM||||I know there are certain rules for what wines go with what foods. Personally, I think it will vary for each person's taste. I think the most accepted standard is red wine with steak, but follow your own tastes other then rules.<P>For me, when I have Italian, something with a rich marinara sauce, I like a good red wine. Something like Principato Rose'. Reds are best served at room temperature.<P>If I am having a steak, I generally like a chilled white wine. Maybe a Piersporter or a Reisling. Zinfandels are often considered a woman's wine, but I like them sometimes. As well as Chardonnay and Merlot.<P>As you can see, I don't follow the 'rules', I just go with my taste and that is what matters anyway. Take opportunity when at a restaurant that serves wine to try different things. I promise they won't laugh at you if you order a red wine with fish. Or visit a winery. Just make sure you eat something between switching wines to 'cleanse the pallet'. Wine does have an aftertaste and you won't get the full effect if you don't get rid of the first one completely.<P>Here is a good article that helps explain what to look for in a wine.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.iexplore.com/activities/food_wine101.jhtml;$sessionid$BMQJSLIAAHQNLTWM42HSGWQ?activity=Culinary" TARGET=_blank>http://www.iexplore.com/activities/food_wine101.jhtml;$sessionid$BMQ JSLIAAHQNLTWM42HSGWQ?activity=Culinary</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 07-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||onfire||07-29-2001||02:53 PM||||I really don't have anything more specific to ask at the moment. I just don't want to look too stupid when I go out to a nice place. Generally Italian dinner, seafood is always good. I didn't even know they made wines for dessert's. I am clueless, when it comes to wine. I just need some general guidelines at the moment. Thanks for your help.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Robert||07-29-2001||04:02 PM||||You might want to take a look here  <A HREF="http://www.wine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.wine.com</A> <BR>My personal favorites are;<BR>Laetitia - Pinot Noir<BR>Chateau St Jean – Chardonnay – Robert Young vineyard (for obvious reasons)<BR>Justin – Cabernet<P>Enjoy;<BR>Robert Young<P><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF">   <P> "By the time you die, you should be so used to <BR>paying taxes that it would be almost second nature <BR>to you."||||reg||10
