Z||000010||LarryB||07-29-2001||09:31 PM||||You'll keep hearing drink what you like, that's rule #1.<P>A lot of different places in the world make pretty good wines.  Places you'll keep seeing include France, Italy, Australia, and Chile.  In the US you'll more commonly see them from Napa or Sonoma Valleys in California or from New York.  Napa or Sonoma wines [b]other than from Sutter Home[\b] are usually a safe bet, and a lot of them are very affordable.  I prefer to avoid the coastal varieties but that's individual taste.  There are some excellent small vineyards in Napa/Sonoma, a couple of the better larger ones include Beaulieu Vineyards and Grgich Hills, although some of the other decent wineries there include Mondavi, Beringer, and Kendall-Jackson.  About the only Napa wine I'd avoid is Sutter Home, although IMO the Coppola wines are [b]WAY[\b] overrated. <P>White wines and most dessert wines are almost always served chilled. Reds are almost always served at room temperature or may be slightly cooler if they were stored in a proper wine cellar. <P>Whites are more properly served with lighter food like fish or chicken or fruits or cheese & crackers, unless they're something like a muscat which is a very sweet dessert wine. <P>Reds are more properly served with heavier fare, like pasta with a heavy or tomato sauce or most red meats.  A light red like a Pinot Noir or maybe a Merlot is appropriate for a heavy fish like a grilled tuna or swordfish steak as well. <P>Some of the more common white or blush wine types:<P>Chardonnay - fruity and slightly sweet white <P>Reisling - another white, a bit drier than a chardonnay<P>White Zinfandel - a sweet blush more appropriate to be pounded down as a standalone drink than to be served with food. <P>Some of the more common red wines include:<P>Beaujolais - early red wines that are released several weeks after the harvest.  Drinkable but its the kind of thing you'd chug down at a barbeque, not drink with a nice meal.  Wineries have unofficial contests to see who gets the first Beaujolais out after the harvest, unlike most other wines that are aged in barrels for 1 or more years before bottling.<P>Pinot Noir - lighter, spicy red<P>Merlot - more full bodied than a pinot noir<P>Cabernet Sauvignon - very full body with a lot of tannin.  Very good with something like prime rib. If you're not used to drinking reds, a couple of glasses of a Cab is a ticket to a bad headache from all the tannin. <P>Port - very heavy and somewhat sweet red wine.  A true port can only come from Portugal (just like real champagne can only come from the Champagne region of France) although there are a lot of fine 'port' wines not from Portugal.  Meant more as an after-dessert or later-in-the-evening drink, goes exceptionally well with fine chocolate or if you're smoking a fine cigar. <P>Remember as well that brandy is just distilled wine. <P>BTW JohnnyK, there are a couple of nice wineries near Niagra Falls on the Canadian side of the border.  If you ever want to do something a bit different with a lady friend she might like a day trip to check it out and sample the wares.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
